This study examines the problem of determining the minimum size of a distance-$r$ dominating set in graphs where an upper bound on diameter is not present. Specifically, we focus on graphs with high girth and low average degrees. First, we establish a lower bound of $\Omega\left(\frac{1}{r^2}\cdot\frac{\bar{d}^3}{\log^2{\bar{d}}}
\right)$ for any distance-$r$ dominating set, and then demonstrate that a distance-$r$ dominating set of size $O\left(\frac{r^2}{\log^2{\bar{d}}}
\right)$ exists when the average degree is $\bar{d}$. Building on this, we show that for high-girth graphs, the lower bound can be improved to $\Omega\left(\frac{1}{r^2}\cdot\frac{\bar{d}^3}{\log^2{\bar{d}}}
\right)$ for all sufficiently large values of $\bar{d}$ and $r$. Additionally, we establish a lower bound of $\Omega\left(\frac{\log^2{\bar{d}}}{r}
\right)$ and provide a distributed algorithm that runs in $O\left(\frac{\log^2{\bar{d}}}{r}
\right)$.
